---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Process - NV Studio Design"
  description="How NV Studio Design takes a residential project from first consultation to finished house. Five phases, clear deliverables, and honest decision points."
>
  <section id="one">
    <div class="inner">
      <header class="major">
        <h1>Process</h1>
      </header>

      <p>
        A residential project has more moving parts than most clients
        expect at the start. This page lays out how we structure the work
        so there are always clear deliverables, a clear decision point,
        and no surprises in the middle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most projects move through five phases. Smaller projects (an
        interior-only renovation, a permit-only engagement) collapse
        these; larger ones take longer in each.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Initial consultation &amp; site review</h2>
      <p>
        We start with a conversation &mdash; usually a short call, then a
        visit to the site. The goal is to understand the brief, walk the
        lot or the existing house, and identify the constraints that will
        shape the project: zoning, existing structure, orientation,
        neighbours, budget.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You provide:</strong> a rough brief, any survey or deed you have, photos of the existing house if it&rsquo;s a renovation.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive:</strong> an honest read of whether the project is feasible, a proposed scope of work, and a fixed-fee proposal for the next phase.</li>
        <li><strong>Decision point:</strong> whether to engage for concept &amp; feasibility.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Concept &amp; feasibility</h2>
      <p>
        We develop a small number of design options &mdash; usually two or
        three &mdash; and test each against the Zoning By-law and the
        site. The output isn&rsquo;t a pretty render; it&rsquo;s a
        buildable scheme with a clear idea of where any variances or
        trade-offs will be.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You provide:</strong> feedback on priorities, a rough budget range, and a decision on the chosen direction.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive:</strong> concept plans, basic massing, a zoning summary, and a recommendation on whether the project can proceed as-of-right or will need a minor variance.</li>
        <li><strong>Decision point:</strong> lock the scheme, or loop back with revised priorities.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Design development</h2>
      <p>
        The chosen scheme is worked through in detail: floor plans,
        sections, elevations, materials, structural ideas, interior
        decisions. We resolve how cabinetry meets the structure, how
        lighting coordinates with the ceiling, and how the envelope
        details will actually be built.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is where architecture and interiors come together. If
        we&rsquo;re doing both, interior decisions happen now &mdash; not
        later &mdash; so they can inform the rough-in.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You provide:</strong> decisions on material direction, fixture and finish preferences, input on interior layouts.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive:</strong> a coordinated design development package &mdash; the house as it will actually be built.</li>
        <li><strong>Decision point:</strong> approval to proceed to permit drawings.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. Permit drawings &amp; approvals</h2>
      <p>
        We produce the drawings the reviewing municipality needs to issue
        the permit and coordinate with structural, mechanical, and other
        consultants as required. If a minor variance is needed, we prepare
        the Committee of Adjustment package and attend the hearing.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You provide:</strong> authorisation as the owner, any existing documents (survey, arborist report, etc.), and timely responses to consultant questions.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive:</strong> a complete permit submission, and ongoing management of the reviewer&rsquo;s comments until the permit issues.</li>
        <li><strong>Decision point:</strong> selection of a builder (if not already chosen) in parallel with permit review.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Construction coordination</h2>
      <p>
        Drawings never answer every question on a construction site. We
        stay engaged through construction &mdash; answering the builder&rsquo;s
        questions, reviewing shop drawings, visiting the site at key
        milestones, and protecting the design decisions that were made on
        paper.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>You provide:</strong> access to the site for reviews, decisions on any change orders the builder proposes.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive:</strong> clarifications, issued-for-construction updates, and design-intent reviews at key phases (framing, rough-in, finishes).</li>
        <li><strong>Decision point:</strong> sign-off at substantial completion.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Timelines</h2>
      <p>
        An honest range: a permit-only project can close in a few months;
        a new custom home from first consultation to issued permit is
        usually closer to a year; complex sites and Committee of
        Adjustment hearings extend that. We give a project-specific
        timeline after the initial consultation.
      </p>

      <ul class="actions">
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}contact`} class="button primary">Book a Consultation</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}architectural-design`} class="button">Architectural Design</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
